# @module[/Std/Random/Prng%]
#  Pure pseudo random number generator Prng@ interface.
#
#  Pure pseudo random number generators have some internal state S@. You can
#  seed the state from an arbitrary integer, transition to the next state, or
#  get the n bits of randomness associated the current state, where n is fixed
#  depending on the pseudo random number generator in use.
#
#  Additional functionality is provided in submodules:
#
#  @def[@l{/Std/Random/Prng/Pure%}]
#   Provides an Rng@ instance from a Prng@ using a pure state monad.
#  @def[@l{/Std/Random/Prng/Assert%}]
#   An assertion to facilitate testing Prng@ implementations.
#
#  The following implementations of the Prng@ interface are also available:
#
#  @def[@l{/Std/Random/Lcg%}] Rand48 linear congruential generator
#  @def[@l{/Std/Random/Lfsr%}] Simple linear feedback shift registers
#
#  For example, imagine you want to use a simple 8 bit linear feedback shift
#  register in a Snake game to randomize where the next bit of food appears.
#  We'll chose Lfsr8 as our pseudo random number generator instance and get a
#  pure state monadic Rng@ wrapper around it:
#
#  @code[fble] @
#   Prng = /Std/Random/Lfsr%.Lfsr8;
#   M@, M(.Monad), Rng .= /Std/Random/Prng/Pure%.Pure(Prng);
#
#  We write an Rng@ agnostic implementation for getting the next food
#  position, which will continue to work if we decide to switch to a different
#  random number generator later on:
#
#  @code[fble] @
#   (List@<Pos@>) { M@<Pos@>; }
#   GetNextFood = (List@<Pos@> snake) {
#     Int@ row := M.do(Range(M, Rng, MinRow, MaxRow));
#     Int@ col := M.do(Range(M, Rng, MinCol, MaxCol));
#     Pos@ pos = Pos@(row, col);
#     Any(snake, EqPos(pos)).?(false: M.return(pos));
#     GetNextFood(snake);
#   };
#
#  The game state stores the current state of the shift register:
#
#  @code[fble] @
#   State@ = *(List@<Pos@> snake, Prng.S@ rand, ...);
#
#  The game transition function passes the state value to get the next food
#  position:
#
#  @code[fble] @
#   State@ s = ...;
#   List@<Pos@> snake = Items(new_head, s.snake);
#   Prng.S@ rand = Exec(StepRand(s.dir), s.rand);
#   s.@(snake, rand, ...);
#
#  If desired, the sequence of random numbers generated can be influenced from
#  user input using the feed function of the interface:
#
#  @code[fble] @
#   (Dir@) { M@<Unit@>; }
#   StepRand = (Dir@ d) {
#     Int@ x = d.?(up: Int|0, down: Int|1, left: Int|2, right: Int|3);
#     Rng.feed(Int|2, x);
#   };

Int@ .= /Std/Int%;

# @type[Prng@] Interface for a pseudo random number generator.
#  @arg[@][S@] The type of the state used by the random number generator.
#  @field[(S@) { S@; }][next] The state transition function.
#  @field[Int@][n] The number of random bits of output you get per state.
#  @field[(S@) { Int@; }][bits]
#   Function to extract the n output bits from the given state. The result is
#   an integer value in the range [0, 2^n).
#  @field[(Int@) { S@; }][seed]
#   Function to produce a state value from an integer value used as seed.
#   Different integer values will tend to produce different states, but
#   otherwise you shouldn't assume anything about the relationship between the
#   integer and the state value.
#  @field[(Int@, Int@, S@) { S@; }][feed]
#   Function to feed n random bits into the state. The first argument
#   specifies the number of bits, the second argument should be an integer
#   value in the range [0, 2^n) with the contents of the bits. The third value
#   is the current state. This produces a new state that is some function of
#   the previous state and the random bits.
#
#  For example, for a 48 bit linear congruential generator with formula:
#
#  @code[txt] 
#   Xn+1 = (a * Xn + c) mod m, where n >= 0
#
#  The state type S@ could be in Int@ representing the 48 bits. The next
#  function implements the transition function shown above. The value of n is
#  32, because you get 32 bits of randomness for eachstate. The bits function
#  takes the most significant 32 bits from the 48 bit number. And so on (see
#  /Std/Random/Lfsr% for complete details).
Prng@ = <@ S@> { *(
    @<S@> S@,
    (S@) { S@; } next,
    Int@ n,
    (S@) { Int@; } bits,
    (Int@) { S@; } seed,
    (Int@, Int@, S@) { S@; } feed
); };

@(Prng@);
